WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.034 --> 00:00:19.210
CS702 term project, phase three. Interactive Debugging and Steering of Multi-Agent AI Systems: re-implementing AGDebugger from CHI 2025, with an error-anchored context extension. Team: Nguyen Nhat Minh and Do Duc Anh. This is the backup recording of the live demo.

2
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:33.832
Teams of LLM agents exchange dozens of messages to solve one task. One wrong step cascades, and a long run ends with a wrong answer or none. Plain logs do not show where it went wrong, or let the developer test a fix without starting over.

3
00:00:34.341 --> 00:00:57.093
Target users: developers building and tuning multi-agent systems. Goal: find the message that derailed a run and steer the team to the correct answer. Tasks: browse and step a long history, send messages, reset to an earlier point and edit the offending message, keep an overview of the branches, and, new in our extension, get the suspects ranked for you.

4
00:00:57.608 --> 00:01:24.728
The system: C1, message history with sending, stepping and queue editing; C2, reset-and-edit with session forking on real checkpoints; C3, an overview visualisation; and E1, error-anchored context assembly. Own implementation on AutoGen 0.7.5 and React. The team under debug is a Magentic-One orchestrator with Coder, Executor and WebSearcher on Claude Haiku 5.5.

5
00:01:49.235 --> 00:01:55.379
A recorded failing run is loaded: history and per-message checkpoints replay without any model call.

6
00:01:55.885 --> 00:02:08.125
C1, the message history. Every runtime message is a card: sender, recipient, type and payload, tool calls included. The flag button records an error judgement for the study log.

7
00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:22.898
The task asks for financial-year visitors divided by instruments. After 228 runtime messages, 125 of them shown as cards, the team gives up. Expected: 6651.7.

8
00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:29.821
Sending: a broadcast to the group or a direct message to one agent lands in the runtime queue.

9
00:02:30.125 --> 00:02:36.029
Run processes the queue continuously, Step processes one message, Drop discards the next.

10
00:02:36.531 --> 00:02:42.771
New: a queued message can be edited before the agents see it, or removed from any position in the queue.

11
00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:47.766
Dropped. The queue is empty again; the agents never saw the message.

12
00:02:53.406 --> 00:03:03.102
C3, the overview: one column per session, one rectangle per message, colour by type, sender or recipient. Legend buttons filter.

13
00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:08.900
Coloured by sender: orchestrator, Coder, Executor, WebSearcher and user.

14
00:03:09.414 --> 00:03:13.110
Coloured by type: tool calls and results stand out from text.

15
00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.316
Hover shows the message; a click scrolls the history to it.

16
00:03:19.313 --> 00:03:37.193
E1, our extension. In the paper, participants spent ten of fifteen minutes just reading before their first edit. The error context panel holds the result of one judge pass over the session, run earlier and stored with the recording: up to five suspect messages, ranked, each with a reason.

17
00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:48.380
The suspects are marked in the overview and on the cards. Here the judge points at the searcher's report that the monthly tables were unusable, and at the orchestrator's instruction that forbade using them.

18
00:03:48.899 --> 00:04:02.651
Clicking a suspect assembles its context: the flagged message, who said what to that agent, the preceding tool calls, warnings in that window, and the evidence the judge cited. At the bottom, a concrete suggested edit.

19
00:04:03.678 --> 00:04:10.470
Edit this message and revert opens the editor on that card, pre-filled with the original text and the suggestion.

20
00:04:10.994 --> 00:04:22.298
We replace the instruction: fetch both monthly visitor reports completely and the facilities page, and do not look for annual accounts. This is the paper's add-specific-instructions strategy.

21
00:04:24.331 --> 00:04:38.563
Reset-and-edit restores every agent from the checkpoint before message twenty-five, re-publishes the edited instruction and forks a new session. The old branch stays viewable. Run or Step now continues the branch live, with real model calls.

22
00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:55.338
The finished branch, saved from a live continuation of this same edit: the team reads the monthly tables, sums them and answers 6651.7.

23
00:04:55.843 --> 00:05:01.747
The overview shows both sessions, the fork marker, and the suspect markers on the failing branch.

24
00:05:02.375 --> 00:05:06.023
The original failing branch is still browsable for comparison.

25
00:05:11.289 --> 00:05:22.761
For the user study the same build runs in three conditions from the URL: reduced, without reset and overview; full; and full with E1. Every action is logged per participant.

26
00:05:23.804 --> 00:05:41.372
Status: C1 to C3 and E1 are complete and integrated. Harness: 29 tasks, 3 natural failing runs so far on Claude Haiku, zero on Claude Opus. Study materials are ready; sessions run in weeks 12 to 15. Thank you.
